But here the exception came from the in-tx pre-check (line 97), meaning B's pre-check saw A's committed plan. Then B throws SCHEDULE_KEY_EXISTS conflict instead of replaying! Wait — but that's the pre-existing code... how did this test pass before? Look at createDraft flow:

```java
Optional<PlanResponse> replay = replayIfPresent(OP_CREATE, req.requestKey(), hash);
if (replay.isPresent()) return replay.get();
try {
    return tx.execute(...pre-check → conflict...);
} catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
    return resolveDuplicate(...);
}
```

Race: B calls replayIfPresent before A commits → empty. B enters tx, pre-check: if A already committed → sees plan → throws conflict SCHEDULE_KEY_EXISTS (ApiException, not DuplicateKeyException) → propagates → test fails. If A hasn't committed → B inserts → DuplicateKeyException → resolveDuplicate → replay OK.

So this is a pre-existing race in the baseline code! The in-tx pre-check should, upon finding existing key, attempt replay resolution instead of directly throwing. Why didn't it fail in earlier runs? Timing — earlier runs B's pre-check happened before A's commit. This is a flaky pre-existing test/bug, exposed now possibly because my changes altered timing (loadPlan now does 3 extra queries, slowing A's commit? Actually A commits after idem insert; the window...).

Hmm wait — did my changes make this more likely? The createDraft path is unchanged. But test ordering/timing with 3 extra test classes changed. Anyway, it's a genuine race in the service: when the in-tx pre-check finds an existing scheduleKey, it should distinguish "same idempotent request already completed" (replay) from "genuinely different request". Fix: inside tx, when findByKey present, check idempotency record: if replay exists for (OP_CREATE, requestKey) → return replay (hash match) or 409 (hash mismatch). If no idem record → throw SCHEDULE_KEY_EXISTS.

I can fix by replacing the in-tx pre-check:

```java
Optional<PlanResponse> replayInTx = replayIfPresent(OP_CREATE, req.requestKey(), hash);
if (replayInTx.isPresent()) return replayInTx.get();
if (planRepo.findByKey(req.scheduleKey()).isPresent()) {
    throw conflict("SCHEDULE_KEY_EXISTS", ...);
}
```

replayIfPresent throws 409 on hash mismatch — correct semantics (same key, different params → 409). This preserves the API test `duplicateScheduleKeyRejected` (different requestKey, same scheduleKey → no idem record → SCHEDULE_KEY_EXISTS 409). And `createIdempotentReplayAndParamConflict` (same key different params → replay check outside tx catches it → 409 IDEMPOTENT_KEY_REUSED). Good.

